#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""compound: the store, the event log and the install of the compound mod.

This file is the only code that reads or writes lessons, skills, the event log and the
install record. The mod (hooks/) calls it for every store operation. Standard library
only; runs on Python 3.9 and later.

LEVELS
    project   <repo>/.claude/compound/lessons/<name>/   <repo>/.claude/skills/<name>/
    user      <COMPOUND_HOME>/lessons/<name>/           <COMPOUND_CLAUDE_DIR>/skills/<name>/
    general   <package>/lessons/<name>/                 <package>/skills/<name>/

    <repo> is COMPOUND_PROJECT when set, else `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` of the
    working directory, else the working directory. <package> is the checkout this file
    really lives in (the installed `compound` is a symlink to it).

LESSON FORMAT
    <dir>/SKILL.md: a `---` line, `key: value` lines, a `---` line, the body.
    Keys written here: name, description, match, match-tools, platform, shell, created,
    updated, origin. A string
    value that is not a plain YAML scalar is written as a JSON string, which is a
    valid YAML double-quoted one; both forms, YAML single quotes and a folded or
    literal block (`description: >`) are read. `--update` edits only the keys it
    changes and leaves every other frontmatter line as it is. `match` is a JSON array
    of Python regular expressions on ONE line; each is compiled with re.MULTILINE (so
    `^` and `$` match at the start and end of every line of a command) and tested with
    search() against the first 20000 characters of the text of a tool call (the
    `command` for Bash, otherwise the JSON of the input). A lesson with `match` is a
    guard. `match-tools` is a JSON array of tool names on ONE line: the tools whose
    calls the patterns are tested against. Without it the patterns are tested against
    Bash calls only, so text that is not a command (a file's content, an agent's
    prompt) is never guarded by accident.
    `platform` and `shell` are the lesson's condition: each is one name, or several
    separated by commas (`platform: darwin`, `shell: zsh, bash`). A lesson applies on a
    machine whose platform is one of the names AND whose shell is one of the names; a
    key that is absent holds everywhere. The platform is COMPOUND_PLATFORM, else
    sys.platform as darwin, linux, windows, or its own name. The shell is the file name
    of COMPOUND_SHELL, else of CLAUDE_CODE_SHELL (Claude Code's own override of the shell
    its Bash tool runs), else of SHELL (the login shell, which is what the Bash tool
    runs); with none of them set the shell is unknown and a lesson that names one does
    not apply. A lesson that does not apply here is listed, and marked so, by `list`,
    `show` and `status`; it is no guard, `find` does not return it and the mod neither
    recalls nor offers it.
    `created` and `updated` are local dates.

THE SWITCH
    <COMPOUND_HOME>/disabled.json: a JSON array of the names of the general lessons this
    user switched off (`compound disable NAME`, `compound enable NAME`). A disabled
    lesson is treated as one that does not apply here. Nothing in the package is
    written. Only a lesson of the general pool has a switch: a lesson of the user's own
    is removed with `rm`.

EVENT LOG
    <COMPOUND_HOME>/events.jsonl, append-only, one JSON object per line, one write()
    per line. `compound log` reads ONE object on stdin and appends it. It fills three
    fields when they are absent: `ts`, `session` (from CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID) and
    `project` (the project root). `type` is required, and is one of the types the table
    below gives to the mod. A type a command of this CLI writes (`learn`, `skip`, `rm`,
    `skill`, `promote`, `candidate`, `use`) is refused with exit 2 and the name of that
    command (CLI_WRITES): an event that settles a debt, or says a lesson was written,
    moved or removed, is in the log because the command ran. That keeps the log whole
    against accident and against a session settling its debt with `log`. It is no
    defence against a process running as the user, which can edit the file.

    Every text an event holds is masked and cut where it is written (`append_event`,
    `masked_event`): the rules of the mod's own `redact` (hooks/safe.ts), rule for rule,
    for whatever looks like a credential, and EVENT_TEXT_CAP characters for one text.
    The fields an event is found by (ts, type, session, project, path, lesson, id, ...)
    are written as they are. The memo's quoted requests are masked the same way.

    Fields every event carries:
        ts        string  UTC, "YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ"
        type      string  one of the names below
        session   string  Claude Code session id, "" when unknown
        project   string  absolute path of the project root

    Fields per type. A name marked * is READ by this CLI; the others are stored and
    shown as they are.

        type      written by        fields
        reuse     mod               lessons* [string]  names of the inventory items
                                                       offered, exactly as `compound
                                                       list --json` names them (a
                                                       script's name is its path
                                                       relative to the project)
                                    prompts* [string]  prompt-log ids offered
                                    words    [string]  the words the prompt log was
                                                       searched for
                                    candidates int     earlier requests put to the judge
                                    prompt_id string   the digest of the prompt
                                    ms*, gather_ms*, judge_ms*  int  milliseconds the
                                                       check took: in all, before the
                                                       judge, and the judge
                                    memo     bool      the verdict came from the memo;
                                                       no model was asked
        guard     mod               lesson*  string    the lesson whose match hit
                                    tool     string    tool name
                                    text     string    the text that was tested
                                    ms*      int       milliseconds the check took
                                    watched* bool      the next call of that tool in
                                                       that agent loop is logged as a
                                                       `retry` event
        retry     mod               lessons* [string]  the lessons whose guards refused
                                                       the loop's call before this one
                                    tool     string
                                    same*    bool      this call is the refused call
                                                       sent again (the same text)
                                    text     string    this call
        recall    mod               lesson*  string    the lesson returned beside a
                                                       failure (one recurrence)
                                    level*   string    the lesson's level, and
                                    path*    string    its directory: which lesson of
                                                       that name it was (see Counting)
                                    tool     string
                                    call     string    the call that failed
                                    error    string    the failure's text
                                    at       string    failure | fix: when it was met
                                    guard    bool      the lesson has a match
                                    after_guard bool   the lesson's guard had refused
                                                       a call in this session before
                                                       (see Ineffective); the CLI
                                                       works this out from the
                                                       `guard` events and does not
                                                       read the field
                                    ms       int       milliseconds the judge took
                                    counted  bool      `log` fills it in: this recall
                                                       is one that counts toward
                                                       ineffective (see Ineffective).
                                                       Stored for the reader; the
                                                       CLI works it out again
                                    ineffective* bool  `log` fills it in when it is
                                                       absent, and prints it: this
                                                       recall counts, the lesson now
                                                       has the limit of them, and
                                                       the session can rewrite it
                                                       (never for a general lesson
                                                       or one left in another
                                                       project). The mod reads it
                                                       from the reply to `log`
        capture   mod               id*      string    a short stable hash; `log` fills
                                                       it in when it is absent
                                    tool     string
                                    failed*  string    the failing call
                                    error    string
                                    fixed*   string    the working call
                                    evidence string    the words of the error the judge
                                                       gave for its verdict
                                    call     string    the id of the failed tool call
                                    agent    string    the subagent whose loop it was,
                                                       null for the main loop
                                    ms       int       milliseconds the judge took
        remind    mod               captures [string]  ids of the unsettled captures a
                                                       session was told about at its
                                                       first prompt
        learn     CLI `add`         lesson*  string
                                    level    string    project | user | general
                                    kind     string    lesson | skill
                                    path     string    the lesson's directory
                                    guard    bool      it has a match
                                    update   bool      --update rewrote it
                                    settles* string    --settles: the capture it settles
        skill     CLI `skill`       lesson*  string
                                    level    string    project | user
                                    path     string    the skill's directory
        rm        CLI `rm`          lesson*  string
                                    level    string    project | user
        skip      CLI `skip`        why*     string
                                    settles* string    --settles: the capture it settles
        refuse    mod               why*     string    debt | strengthen | nudge: why a
                                                       stop was refused
                                    debts    [string]  debt: the failed calls' ids
                                    lessons  [string]  strengthen: the lessons owed a
                                                       rewrite
                                    calls    int       nudge: tool calls in the turn
        judge     mod               moment*  string    reuse | recall | fix: which of
                                                       the three questions was asked
                                    verdict* string    what the model answered. reuse:
                                                       named | nothing | not-substantial.
                                                       recall: named | none. fix:
                                                       fix | known | none. Any of
                                                       them: unanswered (no reply in
                                                       time) | unreadable
                                    ms*      int       milliseconds the model call
                                                       took. THE one definition of
                                                       `ms` for a verdict: `recall`
                                                       and `capture` carry the same
                                                       number for theirs
                                    named    [string]  the lessons, skills or earlier
                                                       requests the answer named
                                    reason*  string    for none, unanswered and
                                                       unreadable: why, a few words
                                    tool     string    recall, fix: the tool whose
                                                       call was judged
                                    prompt_id string   reuse: the digest of the prompt
                                    unquoted int       reuse: names the answer gave with
                                                       no words of the prompt to show
                                                       for them, which were dropped
                                    memo*    bool      reuse: the verdict was taken from
                                                       the memo and no model was asked
                                                       (`ms` is then 0)
        nudge     mod               calls    int       tool calls in the turn
        promote   CLI `promote`     lesson*  string
                                    from     string    level it left; for an automatic
                                                       move (--auto), the project root
                                                       it left
                                    to       string    user | general
                                    path     string    where it is now
                                    url      string    the pull request (general only)
                                    was      string    --as: the name it had
                                    auto     bool      --auto: the mod asked for it;
                                                       `project` is then the project
                                                       of the session that met it
                                    also*    [string]  project roots that keep a
                                                       committed, byte-identical copy
                                                       of the lesson
                                    merged   [string]  project roots whose untracked,
                                                       byte-identical copy was removed
        candidate CLI `promote`     lesson*  string    a lesson --auto left in place:
                                                       git tracks it, or another
                                                       project holds a different
                                                       lesson of its name
                                    from*    string    the project root that holds it
                                    seen_in* string    the project root it applied in
        use       CLI `use`         lesson*  string    the skill a session invoked, as
                                                       `list` names it. The mod runs
                                                       `use` when a skill's prompt is
                                                       expanded (the Skill tool, a
                                                       typed `/name`, a preload)
                                    level    string    project | user | general
                                    kind     string    skill
                                    path     string    the skill's directory
        repeat    mod               times    int       how many sessions made this
                                                       kind of request, this one
                                                       included, when the mod offered
                                                       to make it a skill
                                    prompts  [string]  prompt-log ids of the earlier
                                                       requests it counted
                                    prompt_id string   the digest of the prompt
                                    memo     bool      the verdict came from the memo
        error     mod, `check`      where*   string    hook or step that failed
                                    message* string
                                    lesson   string    `check` only: the guard that
                                                       did not finish in the budget

    A line that does not parse is skipped by every reader and counted by `status`.
    An append to a log whose last line has no newline starts on a new line.

    Counting. `counts` for a lesson is {reuse, guard, recall, learn, use}: the number of
    events of that type whose `lesson` equals its name or whose `lessons` list
    contains it, AND that are about that lesson (`about`). Both fields are read on all
    five types, so a recall that matched two lessons may carry `lessons`. A name is not
    a lesson: two projects may each hold a lesson of one name. A project lesson counts
    the events of its own project: the event's `project`, or, for an event that says
    which lesson it means (`level` and `path`, on recall, learn, use, skill), the
    project that path is in. A user or general lesson is one lesson in every project
    and counts every event of its name, except one that names a project lesson which
    is still where the event says; a lesson that moved to the user level keeps what it
    was counted for. Events are selected by type NAME. A line of a type that is not in
    the table above is shown by `events` and counted nowhere.

    Settling. There is one definition, in `unsettled`, and `status`, `events
    --unsettled`, a bare `skip` and the mod's stop check all read it.
    A `capture` is a lesson owed. It is settled by a later `learn` or `skip` event
    that carries `settles` equal to its `id` (from any session, or from none), or that
    carries no `settles` and comes from the same (non-empty) session. ONE EVENT SETTLES
    ONE CAPTURE: an event that names a capture settles that one and no other, and `add`
    and `skip` refuse (exit 2, listing each id with the first line of its failing call)
    to write an event that names none while their session has more than one capture
    unsettled (`settling`). A line without `settles` written before that rule, from a
    session that owed several, still settles what that session owed.
    A `recall` with `ineffective: true` is a strengthening its session owes for that
    lesson (one per session and lesson: the newest). It is settled by a later `learn`
    with `update: true` for the lesson, by a later `skip` from the same session, or by
    a later `rm`, `skill` or `promote` event for the lesson (its `lesson`, or the
    `was` of a move under a new name): a lesson that is gone or renamed is not owed a
    rewrite.
    `events --unsettled` lists both kinds for the last 14 days, and `status` lists the
    captures under Open. `compound skip --why T` run outside a session (no
    CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID) settles the project's one unsettled capture; with several
    it exits 2 and lists their ids.

    The body of a lesson. `add` takes it from `--body TEXT`, from `--body-file PATH`,
    or from stdin. `--body -` reads stdin to its end. Without a body flag a plain
    `add` reads stdin but never waits for it longer than 2 seconds at a time: a stdin
    that neither speaks nor ends (an open socket) is exit 2. `add --update` without a
    body flag keeps the body and does not read stdin at all; text that is already
    waiting there is exit 2, so a body is never dropped in silence.

    Totals. `status` counts, one per event: `reuse` (reuses offered), `guard` (calls a
    guard stopped), `recall` (lessons recalled), `use` (skills used) and `learn` without
    `update` (lessons recorded), with the time of the oldest event. No duration of a failed call or of its
    fix is logged, so no time or tokens saved is ever reported.

    Words. Text output shows a person a word for each type (EVENT_WORDS: `capture` is
    "owed", `learn` is "recorded", ...) and how long ago an event was; `events` prints
    the type names themselves, and `--json` the stored values.

    Last fired. The types the mod writes are reuse, guard, recall, capture, remind,
    refuse, nudge, judge, repeat, error and retry, and it has the CLI write use; `status`
    reports the age of the newest of them.

    Using. `use NAME` counts one use of a skill: NAME is what Claude Code calls the
    skill a session invoked, a bare name for a skill of the project or the user level
    (the project's first) and `compound:NAME` for a skill of this package. A name that is
    no skill `list` shows, and the package's `learn` and `reuse` (UNCOUNTED_SKILLS),
    write nothing; either way the exit status is 0 and `--json` says `used`.

    Verdicts. A `judge` event is written for every question put to the model, whatever
    the answer, so the rate of each verdict and the model's latency can be read from
    the log. They are many and say nothing by themselves: `status` leaves them out of
    Recent, and they are counted for no lesson. The same holds for a `retry` event.

    Report. `report` reads the log and nothing else, and writes nothing. It counts the
    events in its window (--since, --until, --project; all of the log without them) and
    looks in the WHOLE log for what followed each: the first later event that settles a
    capture or an ineffective recall (`settled_by`), the `retry` event after a `guard`
    event of the same session that names its lesson, a later `recall` of the lesson.
    Every figure is {n, of, pct}: a count over what it is counted among. `pct`, a median
    and a 90th percentile (nearest rank) are null when there are fewer than REPORT_MIN_N
    (10), and the text then says "n is too small" with the n. A refusal is `changed`,
    `same` or `none` (no later call of the tool) only when its `guard` event carries
    `watched`; the others are counted under `unwatched`. A recall counts as `again` when
    a later recall names the same lesson; the recalls that follow the lesson's own guard
    in their session are counted apart. The recalls marked `ineffective` that one event
    settled (or that nothing settled) in one session are one debt, and a debt declined
    with a reason that says the lesson "does not describe" the failure (WRONG_LESSON_RE)
    is counted as the wrong lesson. Declines of captures are grouped by the reason up to
    its first colon, semicolon or full stop (`reason_key`). A `judge` event with `memo`
    is no model call. A duration is a number, or a string of digits. A reuse check that
    finds no candidate writes no event, so the number of checks made is not reported, and
    nothing says whether an offered item was opened or run: `not_measured` lists what
    the log cannot support. An event of a type the report does not read is counted under
    `window.unread` and nowhere else.

    Finding. `find` ranks lessons, skills and the project's scripts by the WEIGHT of the
    words they share with the query, not by their number. Words are compared by a light
    stem (`stem`). A word's rarity is read from the store: 1 when one entry carries it,
    down to 0 when every entry does (`rarity`); a word in COMMON_WORDS counts for a tenth.
    A word found only in a body counts for half, and a body for one word at most. Each
    row carries `weight`, `score` (the number of query words it holds) and `matched`.
    `find --request` reads a whole request on stdin and lists only candidates: weight at
    least the floor (--floor, else COMPOUND_REUSE_FLOOR, else 1.5) and at least a third
    of the best one's. The prompt log is searched for at most ten of the request's words
    (`search_words`), a logged prompt is matched on its first 300 characters, and for a
    request a hit must hold two of the words and a third of their weight (`prompt_hits`). One
    text asked in several sessions is one hit, and its `sessions` names them (its own
    first, at most 20).

    The memo. <COMPOUND_HOME>/memo.json: {"version": 1, "entries": {key: {"at": epoch
    seconds, "verdict": named | nothing | not-substantial, "items": [names], "prompts":
    [{id, ts, project, session, prompt, sessions}], "repeats": [rows like those: the
    earlier requests of the same kind], "asked": [session ids]}}}. `asked` is the sessions
    that asked the request: the one that had it judged, and each one `find --request`
    answered from the memo since, which it adds as it answers (at most 20). `find --request --json` prints the key of a
    request as `memo_key` (a digest of the project, the request's text, the floor, the
    limit and the content of everything it was ranked against, so any change to the
    store changes it) and the entry kept for it, if any, as `memo`. `compound memo`
    reads {"key", "verdict", "items", "prompts", "repeats"} on stdin and keeps it. An entry lives 7
    days and the memo holds the newest 200. Two writers at once may lose one entry,
    which costs one model call the next time.

    What `add` refuses. A new lesson whose text is a copy of one the session can see
    (`duplicate_of`; --new records it anyway), and a description or body that names a
    temporary path or the id of one session, or speaks of "this session"
    (`residue_problem`; --as-written records it anyway). Both are exit 2.

    Ineffective. A lesson is ineffective when at least COMPOUND_RECUR_LIMIT (default
    2) of its recalls COUNT. Every `recall` is logged and shown; the ones that count
    (`counted_recalls`) are about this lesson, have a `ts` later than its SKILL.md's
    modification time, and are AT MOST ONE FOR EACH SESSION: several failures of one
    session (calls sent together, a loop that retries) say once that the lesson did
    not prevent them. Rewriting the lesson (`compound add --update`) is a new revision:
    the count starts over, and the same session counts once more. A `recall` that
    follows a `guard` event of the same lesson in the same (non-empty) session does
    not count: a guard refuses once per session and the call sent again runs, so a
    failure after the refusal is not the lesson failing to prevent it. `show --json`
    says so for the caller's session as `guarded_in_session`. Whether one recall
    counts, and whether it makes its lesson ineffective, is decided in one place,
    `recall_verdict`, when `log` writes the event; `log --json` prints the answer and
    the mod reads it there.

    Recurring. A lesson of the general pool is never ineffective and is never a
    strengthening owed: `add --update` does not rewrite it, so a session could not pay
    that debt. With COMPOUND_RECUR_LIMIT such recalls it is `recurring`: `list`, `show`
    and `status` say so, and `status` lists it under Open with the two things a user can
    do, `compound disable NAME` and a report to the package's repository. `unsettled`
    leaves out a `recall` of a general lesson whatever the event says.

    Several guards on one call. No guard yields to another: every guard in force that
    matches a call is a hit of `check`, whatever its level, and the mod quotes them in
    one refusal. A user who keeps a lesson of their own for a mistake the pool also
    covers switches the general one off (`compound disable NAME`).

WHAT A REPOSITORY CAN WRITE
    A project lesson is a file in a repository, and whoever can commit to it can write
    one by hand. A lesson file is therefore held to the format where it is READ:
      - its directory name is a slug (SLUG_RE), its SKILL.md is at most LESSON_BYTES, it
        holds at most MATCH_MAX patterns of at most MATCH_CHARS characters, and at the
        project level it is not a link that leaves the project's lessons directory.
        Otherwise it is reported under `lessons parse` and is not used. A skill
        directory or a script whose name is not one printable line is not listed.
      - a pattern is compiled in this process and RUN only in a forked child that is
        abandoned at a limit: on the probes when lessons are loaded (VET_BUDGET_MS, see
        vet_patterns), on the call in `check` (COMPOUND_CHECK_BUDGET_MS). In both, the
        user's and the package's patterns go before the project's.
      - a project lesson with the name of a user or general lesson is `shadowed`:
        listed, flagged, and not in force.
    What is printed for a person passes through `one_line`, which drops control
    characters, and a name or a path in a command the CLI prints is quoted (sh_quote).
    `promote --to general` copies what it would publish into a staging directory,
    each file read once and whole (`stage_files`), reads that copy in full for the
    shape of a credential (SECRET_SHAPES), and publishes that copy. With `--yes` it
    refuses when it finds one, and when a file cannot be read or is larger than
    PUBLISH_BYTES: a file that is not scanned is not published. Nothing overrides it.

    A value from outside is never an option of a program this file runs: every program
    is started from an argument vector with no shell, a ref, an owner/repo and a git
    source are held to a pattern first (REF_RE, UPSTREAM_RE, git_source_problem), and
    each goes after `--` where the program takes one.

    One lesson in two places. Two lesson directories of one name whose trees are byte
    for byte the same are one lesson. A project copy identical to the user-level
    lesson of its name is not listed; `status` names it as "also committed in".

ENVIRONMENT
    COMPOUND_HOME        user level root and the event log (default <claude dir>/compound)
    COMPOUND_CLAUDE_DIR  the Claude Code directory (default ~/.claude)
    COMPOUND_PROJECT     the project root, overriding git
    COMPOUND_NOW         pins the clock: epoch seconds or an ISO 8601 time
    COMPOUND_CHECK_BUDGET_MS  wall-clock budget of one `check`, default 500
    COMPOUND_RECUR_LIMIT recurrences before a lesson is ineffective (default 2)
    COMPOUND_PLATFORM    the platform a lesson's `platform` is held against (default: this one)
    COMPOUND_SHELL       the shell a lesson's `shell` is held against (default: CLAUDE_CODE_SHELL, else SHELL)
    COMPOUND_REUSE_FLOOR the weight a candidate of `find --request` must reach (default 1.5)
    COMPOUND_OFF         1 switches the mod off; `status` reports it
    COMPOUND_UPSTREAM    owner/repo `promote --to general` proposes to
    COMPOUND_UPSTREAM_GIT  a git URL or path: `promote --to general --yes` pushes its
                         branch there with git alone (no gh, no fork, no pull request)
    COMPOUND_SURFER      the history-surfer executable (default: `surfer` on PATH)
    COMPOUND_NO_SURFER   install does not fetch history-surfer
    COMPOUND_SURFER_URL  where install clones history-surfer from
    CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID  set by Claude Code; stamps `session` on events written here
    NO_COLOR, TERM       text output is coloured only when stdout is a terminal, NO_COLOR is
                         unset or empty and TERM is not "dumb"; `--json` never is
    COLUMNS              the width text output is fitted to; else the terminal's; piped
                         with neither, lines are not fitted

    COMPOUND_RECUR_LIMIT and COMPOUND_OFF are shared with the mod, which reads them in
    a session where Claude Code has applied the `env` block of its settings. Run from
    a terminal the CLI has no such environment, so it resolves each of the two from
    the process environment first, then from `env` in <claude dir>/settings.json,
    then the default (see `setting`). COMPOUND_PLATFORM and COMPOUND_SHELL are resolved
    the same way, so a terminal and a session agree on which lessons apply.

EXIT STATUS
    0 success; 2 usage or validation error (an unknown lesson name included);
    1 any other failure; 3 from `promote --to user --auto` alone, when the lesson is
    tracked by git in its repository and was left where it is. Errors go to stderr. No command ends in a traceback: an
    unexpected exception is one line, `compound: internal error: <type>: <message>`,
    and exit 1. `check` is the exception: it runs before every tool call, so on any
    internal failure it prints {"hits": [], "error": "..."} and exits 0, and a guard
    that does not finish inside the budget is named in "timed_out".
"""

import fcntl
import json
import os
import re
import sys
import time

SLUG_RE = re.compile(r"^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{1,62}$")
LEVELS = ("project", "user", "general")
